# Ledgerly — environment template
#
# Written by oma-devops (phase 06). NOT a .env file: copy the lines you need
# into `.env` locally, or paste them into your host's secret store. Every value
# below is an obviously fake placeholder or a real non-secret default. There is
# no credential in this file and there must never be one.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW THIS LIST WAS BUILT — it is a grep result, not a memory
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   $ grep -rnE "process\.env|import\.meta\.env|NEXT_PUBLIC_" . \
#       --exclude-dir=node_modules --exclude-dir=.next --exclude-dir=generated
#
# Ten hits, three distinct variable names, and that is the whole surface:
#
#   DATABASE_URL   prisma.config.ts:18, src/server/env.ts:35-37,
#                  src/server/testing/setup.ts:31
#   NODE_ENV       src/server/env.ts:33, next.config.ts:12
#   CI             playwright.config.ts:19,33
#
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_` returns zero hits: this app ships no environment value to the
# browser at all. Confirmed independently by the security review's client-bundle
# check.
#
# Three more variables appear below because Next's standalone `server.js` reads
# them at runtime even though no line of Ledgerly's own source mentions them.
# They are marked FRAMEWORK. Leaving them out is how a container ends up
# listening on 127.0.0.1 inside its own network namespace and failing every
# health check for reasons nobody can see.
# ===========================================================================


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATABASE_URL — required in production, optional locally
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What it is: the SQLite database file, as a `file:` URL. This is the entire
# ledger — clients, invoices, line items, sessions, password hashes.
#
# Validated at boot by src/server/env.ts: it must be `:memory:` or start with
# `file:` and name a file. A malformed value throws on startup rather than
# failing later inside a query, so a bad paste fails loudly and immediately.
#
# Local default: if unset, src/server/env.ts falls back to
# `file:./data/ledgerly.db`, so a fresh clone runs with no .env file at all.
#
# In the container this MUST be an absolute path pointing at the mounted
# volume. A relative `file:./data/...` resolves against the process working
# directory, which means the database lands inside the container's own
# filesystem and is destroyed by the next deploy. This is the single most
# expensive mistake available in this deployment.
#
# Where the real value comes from: you choose it — it is a path, not a secret
# issued by anyone. On Fly.io it is the mount path from fly.toml.
DATABASE_URL="file:/data/ledgerly.db"

# Local development / a fresh clone:
# DATABASE_URL="file:./data/ledgerly.db"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NODE_ENV — required in production
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What it is: `development` | `test` | `production`. Not cosmetic here — three
# real behaviours hang off it:
#   * src/server/auth.ts:23  — the `Secure` flag on the session cookie is set
#                              only when this is `production`. Get it wrong and
#                              the session cookie is transmittable over http.
#   * next.config.ts:12      — HSTS is emitted only when this is `production`.
#   * src/server/db.ts:63    — the dev-only global PrismaClient cache.
#
# Where the real value comes from: set automatically to `production` by the
# Dockerfile's runtime stage and by `next build`. Set it by hand only if you
# are running `node server.js` outside the container.
NODE_ENV="production"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CI — set by the CI runner, never by you
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What it is: any truthy value. Read only by playwright.config.ts, where it
# makes `test.only` a hard failure and stops Playwright reusing an already-
# running dev server instead of booting the build it is supposed to test.
#
# Where the real value comes from: GitHub Actions sets `CI=true` on every
# runner automatically. Do not set it in production; nothing there reads it.
# CI="true"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PORT — FRAMEWORK (Next standalone server.js), optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The port the standalone server binds. Defaults to 3000, which is what the
# Dockerfile's EXPOSE and HEALTHCHECK assume. Change both together or neither.
PORT="3000"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOSTNAME — FRAMEWORK (Next standalone server.js), required in a container
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The interface the standalone server binds. It MUST be 0.0.0.0 inside a
# container: the default binds an address unreachable from outside the
# container's network namespace, and the symptom is a health check that never
# passes with an application log that says the server started fine.
HOSTNAME="0.0.0.0"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEXT_TELEMETRY_DISABLED — FRAMEWORK, optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `1` opts the build and the server out of Next's anonymous telemetry. Set in
# the Dockerfile already; listed here so the value in the image is not a
# mystery.
NEXT_TELEMETRY_DISABLED="1"


# ===========================================================================
# NOT USED — deliberately, so nobody adds them "just in case"
# ===========================================================================
# There is no SESSION_SECRET, JWT_SECRET or signing key. ADR-002: the session
# cookie carries a 256-bit random opaque token and the server stores only its
# SHA-256 — there is nothing to sign, so there is no signing key to leak or
# rotate. If a future change introduces one, it belongs here first.
#
# There is no SMTP / email configuration. The PRD defers outbound email; the
# security review's SEC-006 notes this is also why signup enumeration cannot be
# closed in v1.
#
# There is no API key of any kind. The security review confirmed the server
# makes no outbound requests at all.
